<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for a HARDENED-RUNTIME signature (Developer ID + notarisation).
  The ad-hoc signature build-macos.sh applies by default does not use the
  hardened runtime and therefore does not need this file. It is applied by
  build-macos.sh when TX_SIGN_ID names a real Developer ID identity.

  NOTE FOR EDITORS: no comment in this file may contain two hyphens in a row.
  XML forbids them inside a comment, and while most parsers shrug, the one that
  matters here does not: codesign hands entitlements to AMFI, which rejects the
  whole file with

      Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 9

  and refuses to sign. This comment used to spell out a codesign command line,
  flags and all, and every one of those flags began with the forbidden pair. So
  the command is described in prose instead: sign with the runtime option, a
  secure timestamp, this file as the entitlements, and the Developer ID
  identity. build-macos.sh already does exactly that.

  Every key here is required by QtWebEngine, which embeds Chromium:
    allow-jit                        V8 compiles JavaScript to native code.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory Chromium's allocator maps RWX pages.
    disable-library-validation       macdeployqt copies Qt frameworks that were
                                     signed by the Qt project (or ad-hoc), not
                                     by our team id; without this the loader
                                     refuses them at launch.
  There is deliberately NO app-sandbox key: the terminal is not sandboxed, so
  outbound HTTPS and WebSocket traffic needs no network entitlement.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
